1 CONVERT

   Copies the records of up to 10 files, one after another, to one file,
   changing their organization and format to the output file's.

   Format

     CONVERT input-filespec[,...] output-filespec

2 Parameters

 input-filespec[,...]

   The files to read, with no wildcards.  A host file can be an input,
   each line a record.

 output-filespec

   The file to write, on a mounted volume, with no wildcards.  Without a
   type, it takes the first input's.

2 Description

   CONVERT makes the output file from an FDL file (/FDL), or else with
   the first input file's attributes, unless told to use one that exists
   (/NOCREATE).  An indexed output is sorted by its primary key, and
   loaded fast, by building its indexes directly.

   A record that can't go in is reported and passed over, and CONVERT
   goes on: %CONVERT-I-DUP (a duplicate key), RSK (shorter than the
   primary key), RTL (too long), RTS (too short for fixed records), SEQ
   (out of order under /NOSORT), or VFC (too short to fill the fixed
   control).  /EXCEPTIONS_FILE keeps them, and /EXIT ends there.

2 Qualifiers
/APPEND

   Adds the records to the end of an existing sequential output file.

/CREATE

      /[NO]CREATE

   /NOCREATE uses an existing output file.  /CREATE, the default, makes
   a new one.

/EXCEPTIONS_FILE

      /EXCEPTIONS_FILE[=filespec]

   Copies each record that can't go in to this file (type .EXC), or
   with no file shows it.

/EXIT

   Ends at the first record that can't go in.

/FAST_LOAD

      /[NO]FAST_LOAD

   /NOFAST_LOAD loads an indexed output with RMS's $PUT, a record at a
   time, rather than building its indexes directly.

/FDL

      /FDL=fdl-filespec

   Makes the output from this FDL file (type .FDL).  A warning from it
   stops the conversion.

/FILL_BUCKETS

   Fills an indexed output's buckets, whatever its FDL's fill values.

/FIXED_CONTROL

   Between VFC and other records, moves the fixed control into or out
   of the data, rather than dropping it.

/KEY

      /KEY=n

   Reads an indexed input in the order of key n.

/MERGE

   Inserts the records into an existing indexed file by their keys.

/PAD

      /PAD[=c]

   Fills short records of a fixed-length output with c: a character, or
   %Dn, %On, or %Xn.  The default is a null.

/PROLOG

      /PROLOG=n

   The prolog of an indexed output.  Only Prolog 3 is there: 1 or 2 is
   RMS$_PLV.

/READ_CHECK

   Taken, and does nothing.

/SHARE

   Opens the input files so that other processes may write them while
   CONVERT reads them.

/SORT

      /[NO]SORT

   /NOSORT says the input is in the output's primary key order already.

/STATISTICS

   Shows the counts of records and the time taken.

/TRUNCATE

   Cuts off records too long for the output.

/WORK_FILES

      /WORK_FILES=n

   Taken, and does nothing; n past 10 is SORT's BAD_MERGE.

/WRITE_CHECK

   Taken, and does nothing.

2 Examples

   1.$ CONVERT/FDL=PARTS/EXCEPTIONS_FILE=BAD PARTS.TXT PARTS.IDX

     Makes the indexed file PARTS.IDX from PARTS.FDL, loads it with
     PARTS.TXT's lines, and writes those it can't take to BAD.EXC.

   2.$ CONVERT/APPEND NEW.DAT ALL.DAT

     Adds NEW.DAT's records to the end of ALL.DAT.
